  "image_name": "21edae07fc2b9b115bc79ab21d763c7c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the operating principle of a photovoltaic or photodetector device built from layered semiconductors. On the left, a 3D schematic shows a thin multilayer stack contacted by two top electrodes and connected to an external load. Light appears to excite charge carriers in the active layers, and the separated charges drive a current through the external circuit, producing a measurable voltage.\n\nThe right side explains this with an energy-band diagram across the device. The conduction and valence bands bend through the layered structure, indicating built-in potential differences at the interfaces. Incoming photons promote electrons from the valence band to the conduction band across the band gap, creating electron–hole pairs. The blue electron and red hole are then driven in opposite directions by the internal electric field and band offsets, so electrons move toward one contact while holes move toward the other. This spatial separation reduces recombination and generates photocurrent. A labeled energy difference, shown as qV, represents the photovoltage associated with the separated quasi-Fermi levels or extracted carriers. Overall, the figure emphasizes how the heterostructure’s band alignment enables light absorption, charge separation, and electrical power generation.",
